n enable on the intended input domain. Without such a witness, do not label the\nrule unsound; state the narrower evidence gap instead. Reject rules that encode\nthe task's answer, replace a property-bearing computation with an unconstrained\noracle, bypass real execution, silently fabricate results for unmodeled used\nconstructs, or otherwise make a false claim provable. Distinguish an acceptable\nlow-level trust boundary from a smuggled correctness conclusion.\n\n### 6. Fresh non-vacuity test\n\nInspect any candidate `spec-vacuity.k` only as untrusted evidence. In scratch,\ncreate a fresh, meaningful false mutation that changes a result-constraining\nobligation and is demonstrably false for at least one satisfying input. Require\nthe mutation to build successfully and the proof to fail because of the expected\nunmet obligation. A parser error, missing import, timeout, unrelated crash, or\nmutation that was never reachable is not non-vacuity evidence. Preserve the\nmutation and its build/proof logs.\n\n### 7. proven versus assumed accounting\n\nState precisely what the successful reachability proof establishes. List every\ntrusted primitive, opaque symbol, empirical bridge, and informal argument.\nExplain why each boundary is acceptable, concerning, or illegitimate. Do not\naccept `PROOF.md`, generated traces, or differential testing as a substitute for\nthe K proof; differential evidence may support only the bridge it actually\ntests.\n\n## Decision boundary\n\n- `PASS / LEGIT`: the reconstructed proof is sound, result-constraining, and\n  pins the real generated program, with no material adequacy gap.\n- `CONCERNS / LEGIT`: the reconstructed proof is sound, pins the real generated\n  program, and covers the material source-contract domain, but has a documented\n  non-fatal evidence or trust-boundary limitation. Informal intent bridges, thin\n  differential coverage, and over-broad-but-sound-on-the-intended-domain rules\n  belong here only when they do not make a 